  "query_preview": "..The upper air pattern across Arizona continues to \ntransition into a Monsoon Type IV pattern with the upper high \nto our east and an upper level trof to the NW. This pattern \nbrings an increased threat of severe storms, flash flooding, and\nnocturnal storms this weekend into Monday.\n\nStorms have been forming over the higher terrain early this \nafternoon with further development over the next 3 to 6 hours. Main \nthreat from storms today will be strong gusty outflow winds, some \nwhich may be briefly severe, blowing dust and heavy rain. The strong \noutflow winds may produce areas of blowing dust across Pima and \nPinal counties where a blowing dust advisory is in effect from 4 pm \nto 8 pm. With potential rainfall rates with some of the stronger \ncores being 1-2\"/hr, flash flooding is a threat, especially across \nPinal, Pima and Santa Cruz county where a flash flood watch is in \neffect. Not everyone will see heavy rain or rain at all in the flash \nflood watch area. Tonight, across central Sonora MX near Hermosillo, \na decaying MCS may send a strong outflow north towards the area and \nkick off some elevated storms across the central part of the \nforecast, or mainly across Santa Cruz, eastern Pima and western \nCochise counties.\n\nSunday: after lingering morning convection ends, storms will be \ndeveloping over the higher terrain early afternoon and work their \nway into the lower elevations. Another chance of localized severe \nstorms and flash flooding. Can't rule out storms continuing late \nSunday night into early Monday morning.\n\nMonday: Likely a later start to convection with less areal coverage \nof storms.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday: Low-grade monsoon days as the monsoon \ncalendar turns to September and possible tropical influences \nduring the month.\n\nSpeaking of the tropics, NHC is monitoring an area of disturbed \nweather, associated with a tropical wave, well south of SW Mexico. \nThis may become the next named eastern Pacific tropical storm with \nsome model guidance indicating that the potential track will be \nfurther east than where current Tropical storm Karina is at, and \ncloser to Baja by the end of next week. Confidence low this far out \nbut something to keep an eye on.\n\nTemperature wise, today is the last day of the almost two-week long \nExtreme Heat Warning. Highs will thus be cooler during the next 7 \ndays but still running, except for Monday and Tuesday, above normal \nfor the most part.",
